  <data name="Glossary_Copy.Content" xml:space="preserve"><value>Copy</value></data>
  <data name="Glossary_Format" xml:space="preserve"><value>A glossary is a UTF-8 text file with one entry per line:
• a term, name or proper noun, e.g. Kubernetes. These are given to speech recognition as hints. Put the most important ones first: about 20 are used at a time, and the rest take turns.
• a correction "wrong -&gt; right", e.g. cube cuddle -&gt; kubectl. Applied to every transcript after recognition; the original wording stays in transcript.json.
• lines starting with # are notes.
The shared glossary is Library\Projects\glossary.txt; a project's glossary is glossary.txt in that project's folder. Both are used for recordings in the project.</value></data>
  <data name="Glossary_AiRequest" xml:space="preserve"><value>Please write a glossary for transcribing audio about: [describe the subject, course or meeting here]

Format (plain text, one entry per line, no numbering or explanations):
- Technical terms, people's names, product names, case names and other proper nouns from this field, most important first, at most about 200 lines.
- If you know a likely mishearing, add a line "wrong -&gt; right", e.g. cube cuddle -&gt; kubectl.
- Lines starting with # are comments.</value></data>
  <data name="Detail_GlossaryTip.Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>A glossary can improve accuracy</value></data>
  <data name="GlossaryTip_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>If technical terms or names were written as other words that sound the same, a glossary fixes them. 1) Copy the request and give it to an AI assistant together with transcript.txt from this recording's folder. 2) Save the answer as a .txt file and choose "Import glossary and transcribe again". The glossary is used for every recording in the project "{0}".</value></data>
